 ('REF-00960','journal_article','Ulahannan','Arun','Ulahannan A, Birrell S, Herriotts P',2025,
  'Inclusive streetscapes: Embedding disabled people''s lived experience into street accessibility',
  'Wellbeing, Space and Society','8',NULL,NULL,NULL,'100261',NULL,
  '10.1016/j.wss.2025.100261','https://pure.coventry.ac.uk/ws/files/107128445/1-s2.0-S2666558125000272-main_1_.pdf','2026-07-26',
  3,'clinical','GB','EN','EN','VERIFIED','RESOLVED','crossref+openalex+repository-fulltext','2026-07-26','high',0,
  'Staged in batch 1 from a Consensus abstract as "26 UK interviews on streetscape barriers", with the honest caveat that whether it addressed SEATING was not established. Reading it settled that: it does NOT. Expectation that it would support rest-point provision was WRONG.',
  'Crossref query.bibliographic (Inclusive streetscapes...); OpenAlex OA-location lookup; repository full text',
  'R10 LADDER COMPLETED ACROSS FOUR RUNGS after ScienceDirect returned 403 in batch 2: (1) Crossref by bibliographic query recovered the DOI 10.1016/j.wss.2025.100261; (2) Crossref record confirmed CC BY 4.0, i.e. genuinely open access despite the publisher block; (3) OpenAlex located a green-OA copy in Coventry University Pure; (4) that PDF was downloaded and read in full. A publisher 403 was not treated as a terminal answer.',
  'R15 CORRECTION - THE STAGED DESCRIPTION WAS WRONG ON THE KEY POINT. This paper contains NO finding about seating, benches, rest points or spacing. The word "benches" occurs exactly twice, both times as an EXAMPLE OF STREET FURNITURE in definitional text ("the pavements, trees, benches, bins, public art, lampposts etc.") and in the interview preamble ("anything that''s placed on the street itself like benches or bins"). Had this been admitted on its title and abstract it would have entered the corpus as apparent support for rest-point provision, which it is not. || WHAT IT DOES EVIDENCE, and why it is admitted anyway: n=26 semi-structured interviews with disabled people across the UK, thematic analysis. One of its FOUR key impacts of inaccessible streetscapes is "feeling exhausted" - "There was a recurrent impact of participants feeling exhausted due to the streetscape barriers and challenges raised. At its core was the response that participation in society was draining." Participants described having to "pick their battles" over where to spend energy. That is direct lived-experience evidence for the DEMAND this slug serves (AX-STA sustained-exertion), even though it says nothing about the PROVISION. || The paper also reports that chronic pain and stress directly caused by inaccessible streets are under-recognised: "there has been little recognition of chronic pain and stress, directly caused by inaccessible streets" - relevant to AX-PAI. || TIER CALL: T3, NOT Co-1. Investigator-led academic qualitative research, consistent with the batch-1 treatment of REF-00947. The publisher (National Centre for Accessible Transport, Coventry University) About page was re-retrieved to test whether Co-1 applied: it states ncat "works directly with disabled people, disability organisations, transport providers and policy makers" and "amplifying the voices of disabled people", which describes ENGAGEMENT, not disabled governance, and does not say disabled-led. Same standard applied to Wheels for Wellbeing in batch 1, applied here against my own interest since this slug badly needs Co-1.',
  '2026-07-26','session_2026-07-26-energy-conservation-rest-points-seating-b3'),

 ('REF-00961','journal_article','Holden','J M','Holden JM, Fernie GR',1989,
